      "lines": [
        {
          "runs": [
            {
              "text": "Thesis — Analyst Interpretation. Durable replacement opportunities and strong observed operating profitability can support the debt burden only if operating cash survives working-capital, interest and investment demands and is retained rather than redirected to acquisitions and shareholder distributions. Basis: E1–E7.",
              "kind": "text",
              "ref_id": null
            },
            {
              "text": "C86",
              "kind": "fig",
              "ref_id": "C86"
            }
          ],
          "label": "Thesis",
          "section": "thesis"
        },
        {
          "runs": [
            {
              "text": "Installed-base replacement opportunity — Analyst Interpretation. E2/E7 support recurring replacement needs across proprietary components and multiple applications; qualification friction can make substitution difficult, helping revenue durability and debt-service support. Counterevidence is the absence of generally committed aftermarket volumes and sensitivity to fleet activity, customer finances and procurement. The view would weaken with sustained loss of installed-product positions, qualification problems or deterioration in delivered aftermarket orders; verified long-term demand coverage would strengthen it.",
              "kind": "text",
              "ref_id": null
            },
            {
              "text": "C87",
              "kind": "fig",
              "ref_id": "C87"
            }
          ],
          "label": "Strengths",
          "section": "strengths"
        },
        {
          "runs": [
            {
              "text": "Demonstrated operating profitability and cash generation — Analyst Interpretation. E3/E4 show resilient operating margins and improving annual cash conversion, providing resources for interest and reinvestment before discretionary deployment. Counterevidence is fixed-price cost exposure, working-capital consumption, unvalidated add-back recurrence and excluded acquisition/distribution uses. The view would weaken with persistent margin compression coupled with poor operating-cash conversion, or strengthen with a supported cash-earnings bridge and sustained conversion through weaker demand.",
              "kind": "text",
              "ref_id": null
            },
            {
              "text": "C88",
              "kind": "fig",
              "ref_id": "C88"
            }
          ],
          "label": "Strengths",
          "section": "strengths"
        },
        {
          "runs": [
            {
              "text": "Historical financing execution and reporting base — Analyst Interpretation. E6/E8 document completed refinancings/par repayments and audited annual reporting, supporting a traceable historical analysis and evidence of access then. Counterevidence includes secured-priority changes, acquisition-control exclusions and the unresolved maturity schedule. Reconciled maturities, operative documents and completed control integration would strengthen the view; failed financing execution or adverse reporting/control developments would weaken it.",
              "kind": "text",
              "ref_id": null
            },
            {
              "text": "C89",
              "kind": "fig",
              "ref_id": "C89"
            }
          ],
          "label": "Strengths",
          "section": "strengths"
        },
        {
          "runs": [
            {
              "text": "Cash retention subordinated to discretionary deployment — Analyst Interpretation; key risk severity: Material. E1/E5 document acquisition spending, cash payouts and a debt-funded dividend declaration; transferring resources to owners while debt service remains interrupts organic deleveraging and reduces creditor flexibility. Counterevidence is genuine operating cash generation and no program repurchases in FY2024/FY2023 (CP-1A TX05), neither of which neutralizes actual distributions. Sustained retention and debt repayment without replacement borrowing would improve the view; further leveraged payouts would weaken it.",
              "kind": "text",
              "ref_id": null
            },
            {
              "text": "C90",
              "kind": "fig",
              "ref_id": "C90"
            }
          ],
          "label": "Weaknesses",
          "section": "weaknesses"
        },
        {
          "runs": [
            {
              "text": "Leveraged funding structure makes operating setbacks consequential — Analyst Interpretation; key risk severity: Material. E5/E6 and the checked leverage/coverage figures show a meaningful debt burden relative to non-GAAP earnings; weaker earnings or cash retention would increase refinancing dependence. Counterevidence is improved historical ordinary-EBITDA/net-interest coverage and completed financing transactions. A reconciled funding timetable and retained-cash deleveraging would improve the view; lower serviceability, higher funding cost or restricted access would worsen it. Missing maturity/legal information is a restriction, not proof of a near-term wall or breach.",
              "kind": "text",
              "ref_id": null
            },
            {
              "text": "C91",
              "kind": "fig",
              "ref_id": "C91"
            }
          ],
          "label": "Weaknesses",
          "section": "weaknesses"
        },
        {
          "runs": [
            {
              "text": "Common sector and execution dependencies despite product breadth — Analyst Interpretation; key risk severity: Material. E2/E3/E7/E8 link aerospace use/production, government procurement, qualified suppliers and repeated acquisition integration to orders, margins and cash. Correlated disruption can overwhelm individual product diversification and magnify leverage pressure. Counterevidence includes defense/commercial exposure, multiple components and management's historical aftermarket-stability characterization, which remains unquantified. More granular platform/customer exposure and demonstrated acquired-unit returns could improve the view; qualification failures, delayed deliveries, adverse procurement events or weak acquired-unit cash returns would weaken it.",
              "kind": "text",
              "ref_id": null
            },
            {
              "text": "C92",
              "kind": "fig",
              "ref_id": "C92"
            }
          ],
          "label": "Weaknesses",
          "section": "weaknesses"
        }
      ],
